1. WHAT IS PUBLIC DOMAIN

BOTH source texts are in the public domain, and this project differs from
the other Wroot Press editions on exactly that point: there is no fresh
translation here.

  (a) The Latin of John Milton's De Doctrina Christiana as established in
      the editio princeps edited by Charles Richard Sumner (Cambridge,
      1825).

  (b) Sumner's own English translation of it (Cambridge, 1825), together
      with his prefatory matter and notes.

No claim is made over either. A faithful transcription of a public-domain
text creates no new copyright, and none is asserted here. Anyone is free to
take either text, or both, and do anything at all with them — including
selling them.


2. WHAT IS LICENSED

The following are original work and are licensed to the public under the
Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License
(CC BY-NC 4.0):

  (a) The structured corpus — the original selection, arrangement, and
      encoding of the two texts, including: the book/chapter addressing
      scheme and its slugs; the chunking and paragraph segmentation; the
      Latin–English alignment; the page-offset anchoring to the 1825
      scans; and the transcription corrections recorded in the project's
      review logs.

  (b) The citation index — the extraction, parsing, normalization, and
      resolution of every scriptural reference in both layers to a book and
      verse; the stable deep-link scheme over them; the paired records
      identifying where the two layers print different numbers; and the
      classification of those divergences.

  (c) The Junius–Tremellius division data — the recorded chapter divisions
      of the Junius–Tremellius Bible (Hanau, 1603), with the leaf read and
      the evidence for each, together with the software that maps them onto
      the citation index.

  (d) The editorial text: headnotes, chapter notes, and all introductory and
      explanatory prose on the site, including the About and Rights pages.

  (e) The site design, typography, stylesheets, and build scripts.

Item (a) is claimed as a compilation and database — the arrangement and the
encoding, not the underlying text. Extracting the 1825 text is free (§1);
extracting this project's structure, alignment, index, and division data is
not.


3. WHAT CC BY-NC 4.0 PERMITS

You may copy, redistribute, excerpt, translate, remix, and build upon the
licensed material, in any medium or format, for NON-COMMERCIAL purposes,
provided you give appropriate credit, link to this license, and indicate if
